   233	in the charge and is not silently assumed here.
   234	
   235	Equations (DEG), (1.3), and (POLY), with the equivariant constants of section
   236	2 and NO-RESIDUE of section 3, are the exact global conditions.
   237	
   238	## 2. What is local, what Galois symmetry supplies, and what is global
   239	
   240	### 2.1 Exact tree factorization
   240	### 2.1 Exact tree factorization
   241	
   242	Let `T_g` be the rooted cluster tree of the `n` roots.  For a leaf `i` and an
   243	internal vertex `v` on its path to the root, let `S_v(i)` be the leaves in the
   244	children of `v` other than the child containing `i`.  Every `j!=i` lies in
   245	exactly one such `S_v(i)`.  Therefore
   246	
   247	```text
   248	 D_i = product_(v on path(i)) product_(j in S_v(i))(tau_i-tau_j),             (2.1)
   249	
   250	 sum_(r>=0)e_r^(i) Z^r
   251	     = product_(v on path(i)) product_(j in S_v(i))(1+Z tau_j).               (2.2)
   252	```
   253	
   254	These two factorizations are exact, not leading-order analogies.  Substitution
   255	in (1.3) proves immediately that every Lagrange coefficient sees every level:
   256	the denominator contains the first separation of `i` from every other leaf,
   257	and the numerator contains all the same sibling packets.
   258	
   259	If a bottom disc `B` has radius `delta_1`, centre `w_B`, and `a_1=eV_2(B)`
   260	roots
   261	
   262	```text
   263	 tau_i=w_B+c_i t^delta_1+higher,       i in B,
   264	```
   265	
   266	then the part of (2.1) internal to `B` begins with
   267	
   268	```text
   269	 t^((a_1-1)delta_1) p_(g,B)'(c_i),
   270	 p_(g,B)(pi)=product_(i in B)(pi-c_i).                         (2.3)
   271	```
   272	
   273	The other factors in (2.1) are products at the ancestor radii.  They are units
   274	after their forced powers are removed, shared by all bottom descendants of
   275	the relevant ancestor, and begin contributing corrections at the positive
   276	junction gaps `|delta_bottom-delta_ancestor|`.  This is precisely the outer-factor contribution
   277	which the hostile review found missing from the restricted local recurrence.
   278	
   279	### 2.2 The genuinely local block and BOTTOM-ODE
   280	
   281	In the completed chart of one bottom disc, put
   282	
   283	```text
   284	 x=t^-1,  y=w_B(t)+pi t^delta_1,
   285	 f=t^lambda_f Phi,       g=t^lambda_g Gamma.
   286	```
   287	
   288	The reviewed coordinate calculation and D1-PIN give the exact necessary
   289	identity for an existing Keller pair
   290	
   291	```text
   292	 (lambda_f Phi+t Phi_t)Gamma_pi
   293	   -(lambda_g Gamma+t Gamma_t)Phi_pi = -c,                    (LOCAL-KELLER)
   294	```
   295	
   296	because `lambda_f+lambda_g=delta_1-1`.  Its leading term, with
   297	`p_f=Phi_0`, `p_g=Gamma_0`, is
   298	
   299	```text
   300	 d p_f p_g' - e p_g p_f' = kappa,
   301	 deg p_g=eV_2,  deg p_f=dV_2,
   302	 kappa=-ec/lambda_g=c(d+e)/(1-delta_1)=cde/q !=0.             (BOTTOM-ODE)
   303	```
   304	
   305	This is **local to one completed bottom disc**.  It implies the reviewed
   306	squarefreeness, coprimality, STAR-RESIDUE, STAR-SUM, and dessin conditions.
   307	In the interpolation language, the leading local star values interpolate to
   308	`p_f`; differentiating the branch values by (JF) supplies the displayed
   309	Wronskian.  Thus the leading local block of the global branchwise system
   310	reproduces BOTTOM-ODE exactly.
   311	
   312	What does not follow is the converse used in the refuted lane: an arbitrary
   313	solution of LOCAL-KELLER in `C[[t^(1/R)]][pi]` need not be the restriction of
   314	a polynomial in `C[x,y]`.  Equations (DEG) and (POLY) are the missing global
   315	conditions.
   316	
   317	### 2.3 Exactly what Galois symmetry gives for free
   318	
   319	Let `Gamma=mu_R` act by `z -> omega z` and permute the roots.  Once
   320	NO-RESIDUE holds, primitives can be chosen equivariantly.  Indeed the
   321	difference between two conjugate primitives is constant, and averaging over
   322	the finite group removes the resulting additive cocycle.  With that choice,
   323	
   324	```text
   325	 gamma(tau_i)=tau_(gamma i),  gamma(F_i)=F_(gamma i),
   326	 a_(gamma i)=a_i.                                             (2.4)
   327	```
   328	
   329	Consequently there is exactly one free integration constant per Galois orbit
   330	of branches.  If `O` is such an orbit, each orbit contribution
   331	
   332	```text
   333	 M_(r,O)=sum_(i in O)H_i tau_i^r/D_i
   334	```
   335	
   336	is `Gamma`-invariant.  Therefore
   337	
   338	```text
   339	 [z^k]M_(r,O)=0 unless R divides k,                            (2.5)
   340	```
   341	
   342	and the analogous assertion holds for the orbit contribution to every
   343	`A_q`.  This is the exact automatic cancellation of fractional powers.  It
   344	occurs **inside each complete Galois orbit**; cancellation between unrelated
   345	orbits is not needed for descent.
   346	
   347	The stabilizer of a bottom disc gives more local structure.  If its image on
   348	the centred coordinate is `pi -> chi pi` of order `Delta`, monicity gives
   349	
   350	```text
   351	 p_g(chi pi)=chi^a p_g(pi),       p_f(chi pi)=chi^b p_f(pi),
   352	 a=eV_2, b=dV_2.                                             (2.6)
   353	```
   354	
   355	Thus their supports lie in one residue class modulo `Delta`.  The nonzero
   356	constant Wronskian gives `Delta | a+b-1`; for `Delta>1` it gives the reviewed
   357	zero-root dichotomy.  Since the roots of `p_g` are simple, its nonzero roots
   358	come in `Delta`-cycles and at most one root is zero, hence
   359	`a=0 or 1 (mod Delta)`.  These are Galois/star constraints, not additional
   360	copies of the global interpolation equations.
   361	
   362	Symmetry gives only the following for free:
   363	
   364	* conjugate discs have conjugate, hence redundant, local equations;
   365	* constants and tame coefficients are identified according to their orbit;
   366	* nontrivial-character, fractional-power terms have zero orbit trace;
   367	* the support congruences such as (2.6) hold.
   368	
   369	It does **not** force the coefficient of `t^h` for an integer `h`, does not
   370	relate two distinct Galois orbits, and does not turn a formal tail into a
   371	polynomial tail.
   372	
   373	### 2.4 The genuinely global blocks
   374	
   375	The following conditions cannot be checked in one bottom chart.
   376	
   377	1. Every moment in (DEG) is a sum over all `n` roots.  Its invariant
   378	   coefficient may cancel between different Galois orbits; no symmetry forces
   379	   that cancellation.
   380	2. Formula (1.3) uses `e_r^(i)` and `D_i`, whose tree factorizations (2.1)-(2.2)
   381	   contain siblings at all levels.  At a level junction the same outer unit is
   382	   seen by several descendant bottom discs, so their nominal local parameters
   383	   are shared.
   384	3. Each polynomial-tail equation (1.11) is an invariant sum over all branch
   385	   orbits.  A separate local polynomial in `pi` can have an infinite `t`-tail
   386	   even when LOCAL-KELLER holds.
   387	4. The finite set of orbit constants `a_O` must solve every order of all these
   388	   identities simultaneously.  A different constant for every formal branch
   389	   is not permitted.
   390	
   391	This is the corrected replacement for DISC-COUPLING: first take the trace over
   392	each Galois orbit, then sum the invariant orbit contributions, retaining the
   393	outer factors from every ancestor.  No resonance pattern is assumed.
   394	
   395	## 3. NO-RESIDUE at all orders
   396	
   397	### 3.1 Branchwise equation
   398	
   399	Write
   400	
   401	```text
   402	 1/D_i = rho_i x^-1 + (terms with exponent != -1).
   403	```
   404	
   405	Since `c!=0`, a Puiseux primitive in (1.1) exists exactly when
   406	
   407	```text
   408	                 rho_i=[x^-1](1/D_i)=0                       (NO-RESIDUE)
   409	```
   410	
   411	for every branch.  In `z=t^(1/R)` this is `[z^R](1/D_i)=0`, because
   412	`dx=-R z^(-R-1)dz`.  All other monomials integrate inside the Puiseux field;
   413	the `z^R` term alone produces `log z` (equivalently `log x`).  Galois conjugate
   414	branches have the same residue, so there is one independent scalar equation
   415	per Galois branch orbit, although a fail-closed program may print all `n`.
   416	
   417	This is an all-orders statement in the sense relevant here: the full product
   418	`D_i` includes every Puiseux coefficient capable of contributing to `z^R`.
   419	A leading valuation check is insufficient if higher products can reach that
   420	exponent.  The truncation guard in the driver is chosen from the valuation of
   421	`D_i^-1`; an input that stops before the residue coefficient is known fails
   422	closed.  More explicitly, if `gamma_i=ord_t D_i`, then `D_i^-1` begins at
   423	`t^-gamma_i` and the residue lies at relative depth
   424	`max(0,1+gamma_i)`.  At a proper frontier the reviewed identity
   425	`gamma_i=-delta_i^0` makes this `max(0,1-delta_i^0)`.
   426	
   427	### 3.2 Its exact logical position inside the global system
   428	
   429	If residues are not set to zero, write a primitive in the differential
   430	extension as
   431	
   432	```text
   433	 H_i=H_i^P+c rho_i log x.
   434	```
   435	
   436	The logarithmic part of the Lagrange interpolant is
   437	
   438	```text
   439	 c log x * R(y),       R(y)=sum_i rho_i ell_i(y),  deg_y R<n. (3.1)
   440	```
   441	
   442	Suppose the high coefficients satisfy (DEG) **including their logarithmic
   443	parts**, and the remaining `m+1` coefficients satisfy (POLY), which excludes
   444	logs.  Then every coefficient of `R(y)` is zero.  Evaluation at `y=tau_i`
   445	gives `R(tau_i)=rho_i`, so every residue is zero.  Therefore:
   446	
   447	```text
   448	 (DEG)+(POLY), interpreted in K[log x], implies NO-RESIDUE.    (3.2)
   449	```
   450	
   451	On the other hand, (POLY) for the lower `m+1` coefficients alone does not
   452	control a logarithm sitting in a high coefficient.  More importantly, the
   453	usual algorithm defines `F_i` in the Puiseux field before it forms the
   454	interpolant.  In that implementation NO-RESIDUE is an independent
   455	well-formedness equation and must be imposed first.  Thus it is logically
   456	redundant in the full log-aware global system, but computationally independent
   457	and not replaceable by a local regularity assertion.
   458	
   459	## 4. The finite-order algorithm and exact counts
   460	
   461	### 4.1 Why the bare Moh tuple has no exact unknown count
   462	
   463	**PROVED-HERE / UNREVIEWED (information obstruction).**  There is no function
   464	
   465	```text
   466	 U_Q=U_Q(n,m,M_*,V_*,delta_*,u,v)
   467	```
   468	
   469	which equals the number of independent tame unknowns through order `Q` for
   470	every realisation of that skeleton.
   470	every realisation of that skeleton.
   471	
   472	The reason is visible without a dimension computation.  The promoted skeleton
   473	data give only a capacity such as
   474	
   475	```text
   476	                  sum_B V_2(B) <= u;                          (4.1)
   477	```
   478	
   479	they do not give the attained packet of bottom discs.  The reviewed `D=105`
   480	row, for example, has `u=20,V_2=1`; `k=20` is permitted, not attained.  A
   481	one-disc packet and a twenty-disc packet have different sets of local tail
   482	coefficients and different junction sharing, yet the printed Moh tuple is the
   483	same.  Even after `k` is supplied, the tuple does not say whether those discs
   484	form one Galois orbit or several, so it does not determine the number of
   485	integration constants or the number of conjugate equations.  Finally, as the
   486	hostile review stressed, `delta_1-delta_2` being a displayed outer activation
   487	does not prove that it is the first exponent in every inner tail.  The tame
   488	support semigroup is also missing.
   489	
   490	The level numbers along one distinguished chain,
   491	
   492	```text
   493	 a_r = n V_(r+1)/d_(r+1),       b_r = m V_(r+1)/d_(r+1),      (4.2)
   494	```
   495	
   496	give cluster sizes, not the full set partition of all `n` leaves.  Therefore a
   497	closed number asserted from the bare tuple would conflate a representative
   498	chain with the full actual branch packet, contrary to the campaign guardrail.
   499	
   500	The minimal exact finite input is a **decorated skeleton to order `Q`**:
   501	
   502	```text
   503	 D_Q = (bare Moh tuple,
   504	        rooted cluster tree on n labelled leaves,
   505	        cyclic Galois action and orbit/stabilizer data,
   506	        truncated Puiseux template tau_i(z) for every leaf,
   507	        declarations identifying shared tame coefficients,
   508	        target order Q and sufficient valuation guard).         (4.3)
   509	```
   510	
   511	This is not extra geometry invented by the algorithm.  It is precisely the
   512	data needed to say what the phrase “the tame coefficients as unknowns” means.
   513	The shipped emitter therefore requires this finite branch decoration and
   514	refuses a bare skeleton rather than reporting it as an exact branch system.
   515	
   516	### 4.2 Order-by-order construction
   517	
   518	Choose `z` so that `x=z^-R`, with `R` divisible by every denominator in the
   519	decorated data.  For a requested last exponent `Q`, do the following.
   520	
   521	1. **Validate custody of the packet.**  Check the number of roots, leading
   522	   separations at every declared tree vertex, closure under the permutation
   523	   `z->omega z`, the declared coefficient identifications, and separability.
   524	   A partial Galois orbit is an input error, not a failed Jacobian equation.
   525	2. **Build all-level denominators.**  Form every difference and the products
   526	   `D_i=product_(j!=i)(tau_i-tau_j)`, retaining valuations rather than dividing
   527	   by a raw truncated polynomial.  Formula (2.1) records which level supplies
   528	   each factor.
   529	3. **Invert with a guard.**  Compute `W_i=D_i^-1` far enough to know every
   530	   coefficient through `z^(Q+R)` needed below.  Unit leaders are recorded and
   531	   saturated/nonzero.  If the supplied root series do not determine that
   532	   range, stop with `INSUFFICIENT-GUARD`.
   533	4. **Impose NO-RESIDUE.**  Emit `[z^R]W_i=0`, orbit-reduced if requested.
   534	5. **Integrate coefficientwise.**  If
   535	   `H_i=sum_q h_(i,q)z^q`, then
   536	
   537	   ```text
   538	        q h_(i,q) = -c R [z^(q+R)]W_i,       q != 0,           (4.4)
   539	   ```
   540	
   541	   while `h_(i,0)=a_O` for the Galois orbit `O` of `i`.  Equation
   542	   (4.4) also shows directly why `[z^R]W_i=0` is the unique logarithmic
   543	   obstruction.
   544	6. **Form symmetric conditions.**  Compute either (1.3), or the moments
   545	   `M_0,...,M_(n-1)`.  Emit (DEG) coefficient by coefficient in increasing
   546	   `z`-order.  After (DEG), polynomiality may equivalently be tested on
   547	
   548	   ```text
   549	                 M_(n-m-1),...,M_(n-1) in C[x].               (4.5)
   550	   ```
   551	
   552	   This is because (1.6), restricted to the remaining `m+1` entries, is a
   553	   unit-triangular transformation with `e_j in C[x]`.  Formula (4.5) is often
   554	   cheaper than explicitly expanding every `e_r^(i)`.
   555	7. **Project characters before counting.**  Check that nontrivial Galois
   556	   characters cancel.  Then emit only the invariant, integer-order equations.
   557	   Sum the surviving invariant contributions across distinct orbits; do not
   558	   set each orbit contribution to zero separately.
   559	8. **Sort by level junction.**  In a bottom chart, mark
   560	
   561	   ```text
   562	       J(edge)=R|delta_child-delta_parent|,
   563	       J_r=R|delta_bottom-delta_r| for a bottom-to-ancestor table.             (4.6)
   564	   ```
   565	
   566	   At `J_r` activate the outer factors and shared symbols belonging to level
   567	   `r`.  The absolute value makes this independent of the two opposite level
   568	   index conventions present in the charge and frozen examples.  The equation
   569	   order is still its actual `z`-order; the junction label is provenance, not
   570	   a replacement order.
   571	
   572	All arithmetic in these steps is finite for fixed `Q`.  Failing at a finite
   573	order proves failure of the all-order condition.  Passing says only “survives
   574	through `Q`”.
   575	
   576	### 4.3 Closed-form intrinsic condition counts
   577	
   578	Let `h` be the number of Galois orbits on branches.  For an invariant Laurent
   579	series `S`, let `ell(S)` be its first retained `z`-exponent, and define
   580	
   581	```text
   582	 N_R(a,Q) := max(0, floor(Q/R)-ceil(a/R)+1).                  (4.7)
   583	```
   584	
   585	There are `d_0:=n-m` moment identities in (DEG), including the monic one.  If
   586	`ell_r=ell(M_r-target_r)`, their exact raw coefficient count through `Q` is
   587	
   588	```text
   589	 C_deg(Q) = sum_(r=0)^(d_0-1) N_R(ell_r,Q).                  (4.8)
   590	```
   591	
   592	For the remaining coefficient `A_j`, let `beta_j` be its first retained
   593	exponent.  The monic series identity `A_m=1` has already killed every tail of
   594	`A_m`; counting it again as polynomiality would duplicate an equation.  For
   595	the additional coefficients `A_0,...,A_(m-1)`, after Galois descent the
   596	forbidden polynomial tail consists of the positive multiples of `R`, so
   597	
   598	```text
   599	C_poly(Q) = sum_(j=0)^(m-1)
   600	   max(0, floor(Q/R)-max(1,ceil(beta_j/R))+1).                (4.9)
   601	```
   602	
   603	Before descent, replace the last count by the cardinality of all exponents in
   604	the window which are not in `-R Z_>=0`; those equations are covariance checks
   605	as well as polynomiality equations.  The independent residue count is
   606	
   607	```text
   608	 C_res=h                                                        (4.10)
   609	```
   610	
   611	once the guard reaches `z^R`; branchwise output contains `n` equations but
   612	`n-h` are Galois repetitions.  Thus the intrinsic raw count is
   613	
   614	```text
   615	              C(Q)=C_res+C_deg(Q)+C_poly(Q).                 (4.11)
   616	```
   617	
   618	This counts equations, not their rank or ideal height.
   619	
   620	If every identity is represented on one common finite invariant exponent set
   621	`Lambda` and `Lambda_bad` is its forbidden positive-order subset, the same
   622	deduplicated count has the compact form
   623	
   624	```text
   625	 C=h+(n-m)|Lambda|+m|Lambda_bad|.                            (4.11a)
   626	```
   627	
   628	At an allowed order there are `n-m` degree/monic equations; at a forbidden
   629	order the `m` lower coefficients add to a total of `n`.  The residue term `h`
   630	is included once, not once per series order.
   631	
   632	A useful normalized special case starts after all earlier leading orders have
   633	been discharged and retains the invariant `t`-orders `0,1,...,K`.  Every
   634	moment identity is checked at `K+1` orders; after deduplicating the already
   635	monic `A_m`, polynomiality adds `K` positive orders for each of the `m` lower
   636	coefficients.  Then
   637	
   638	```text
   639	       C(K) = h + (n-m)(K+1) + mK
   640	            = h + (n-m) + nK.                                (4.12)
   641	```
   642	
   643	The same formula at a level junction uses `Q=J_r` in (4.8)-(4.11).  Notice
   644	that if `J_r` is not divisible by `R`, the first fractional outer activation
   645	need not add a global invariant equation at that exact exponent: its conjugate
   646	orbit sum is zero by (2.5).  It does add active symbols and can enter later
   647	integer orders through products.  This is why a local “one condition per disc
   648	at the first resonance” is not a valid global count.
   649	
   650	### 4.4 Closed-form unknown counts for the decorated skeleton
   651	
   652	Let `Z_Q` be the set of independent declared tame symbols which valuation
   653	arithmetic says can affect an equation through `Q` (including the inverse and
   654	integration guard), and let `u_0` count unspecialized leading/star parameters.
   655	Then, with `c` normalized, the intrinsic unknown count is exactly
   656	
   657	```text
   658	              U(Q)=h+u_0+|Z_Q|.                              (4.13)
   659	```
   660	
   661	If `c` is an unknown nonzero scalar, add one and saturate at `c`; if affine
   662	coordinate or scalar normalizations have not been fixed, list their gauge
   663	dimension separately rather than pretending that it is an equation.
   664	
   665	For a coefficient order `q`, let `A_q` be the set of active coefficient slots
   666	before Galois identification.  In the free-tail model,
   667	
   668	```text
   669	 p_q=|A_q/Gamma|,        |Z_Q|=sum_(q active through Q)p_q.   (4.14)
   670	```
   671	
   672	Declared sharing across descendants replaces orbit classes by the indicated
   673	equivalence classes.  At a junction, (4.14) gives the exact increment: count
   674	the newly active child slots modulo Galois and shared-outer identifications.
   675	This is the requested closed form at every level junction.  It is a function
   676	of the decorated tree; equations (4.1)-(4.2) prove why it cannot be reduced to
   677	the bare tuple.
   678	
   679	### 4.5 The first possible overdetermined order — only a counting bound
   680	
   681	Define
   682	
   683	```text
   684	 Q_count := min{Q in the ordered invariant/junction support : C(Q)>U(Q)},     (4.15)
   685	```
   686	
   687	after fixing gauges consistently on both sides.  This is the first order at
   688	which the number of printed intrinsic equations can exceed the number of
   689	unknowns.  It is a **COUNTING-BOUND**, not a kill theorem.
   690	
   691	In the stationary normalized model `|Z_K|=u_0+pK`, (4.12) gives, when
   692	`n>p`,
   693	
   694	```text
   695	 K_count=max(0, floor((u_0-(n-m))/(n-p))+1).                  (4.16)
   696	```
   697	
   698	If `n<=p` and the inequality does not already hold at `K=0`, this linear
   699	count alone never crosses.  Formula (4.16) is conditional on the stationary
   700	support hypothesis and is printed as such.
   701	
   702	A “kill by counting” needs substantially more than (4.15): on every surviving
   703	component, after saturating all discriminants, denominator leaders, `c`, and
   704	degree leaders, one must prove that the new equations have ideal height (or
   705	independent transverse rank) greater than the available component dimension.
   706	One must first remove Galois repetitions, algebraic identities such as (1.8),
   707	gauge directions, and auxiliary variables.  More equations than variables can
   708	be duplicate or identically zero; only an empty saturated ideal, an explicit
   709	incompatible coefficient, or a valid componentwise codimension proof kills.
   710	
   711	### 4.6 Why the emitted system can be linear/quadratic exactly
   712	
   713	The intrinsic expressions contain products of up to `n-1` differences.  The
   714	driver does not discard higher-degree monomials.  It introduces named
   715	auxiliaries and uses the following quadratic straight-line lift:
   716	
   717	```text
   718	 difference nodes                  linear;
   719	 product chains for D_i            P_new=P_old*(tau_i-tau_j);
   720	 inverse coefficients              D_i W_i=1;
   721	 NO-RESIDUE                         [z^R]W_i=0;
   722	 integration                       q h_(i,q)=-cR w_(i,q+R);
   723	 powers of a root                  T_(i,r)=tau_i*T_(i,r-1);
   724	 interpolation/evaluation          H_i=sum_(r=0)^m A_r*T_(i,r);
   725	 monic normalization               A_m=1.                      (4.17)
   726	```
   727	
   728	Every multiplication equation is bilinear.  Polynomial coefficient variables
   729	`A_r` are created only on the allowed support (1.9), so the evaluation equation
   730	simultaneously imposes the degree and polynomiality conditions.  Conversely,
   731	given the original products and interpolant all auxiliaries have their stated
   732	values.  Thus the lift is existentially equivalent at the retained order; it
   733	is not a quadratic approximation and does not claim that the eliminated ideal
   734	is generated by quadrics in the original tame variables.  The driver reports
