   137	## Orbit Size Proof
   138	
   139	Moh's conditions (8)-(11) are on p.201:13-39. For a hypothetically existing
   140	tower `D_s superset ... superset D_1`, line p.201:19 computes the radii from
   141	Def. 5.1. Lines p.201:20-23 define `A_{r-1}` as the reduced denominator of
   142	`L delta_{r-1}`, where `L` is the l.c.m. of the reduced denominators of
   143	`delta_s,...,delta_r`. Lines p.201:25-30 give the division
   144	
   145	```text
   146	Q := V_r (d_{r-1}/d_r) = Delta_{r-1} A_{r-1} + Square_{r-1}
   147	```
   148	
   149	and the automorphism `tbar -> omega tbar` with `omega` an `A_{r-1}`-th root of
   150	unity. Lines p.201:32-39 split the alternatives: (10) for a factor
   151	`pi-a, a != 0`; (11) for a factor `pi`.
   152	
   153	The freeness issue checks out. Work over the previous field
   154	`k((t^{1/L}))`, so all earlier centre terms are fixed. Write
   155	`L delta_{r-1}=B/A` with `A=A_{r-1}` and `(A,B)=1`; then
   156	`t=(tbar)^{LA}` and `t^{delta_{r-1}}=(tbar)^B`. The automorphism sends the
   157	new coefficient by `pi -> omega^B pi`. Since `(A,B)=1`, `omega^B` is primitive
   158	of order `A`. If `a != 0`, then `(omega^B)^i a=a` implies `(omega^B)^i=1`,
   159	hence `A | i`. The orbit has exactly `A` elements.
   160	
   161	This also answers both hostile edge cases:
   162	
   163	* If `a` is itself a root of unity of smaller order, it does not matter. The
   164	  stabilizer condition is multiplicative cancellation by nonzero `a`, not a
   165	  comparison of orders of `a`.
   166	* If `A` shares factors with previous ramification, those factors are already
   167	  in `L`; the new action is over `k((t^{1/L}))`, and the reduced denominator
   168	  condition `(A,B)=1` makes the new multiplier primitive.
   169	
   170	For the zero factor `pi`, the centre is fixed. The rest of `p(pi)` is a union
   171	of free nonzero orbits, so `Q-V_{r-1}` is divisible by `A`; this is exactly
   172	condition (11). For a nonzero factor, one entire orbit consumes
   173	`A V_{r-1}` degree in `p`, giving condition (10).
   174	
   175	Therefore one bottom-major disc in one tower has
   176	
   177	```text
   178	|O|(D_1) = product_{j=2}^{s-1} omega_j,
   179	omega_j = A_j on (10), omega_j = 1 on (11).
   180	```
   181	
   182	When both (10) and (11) hold at a level, both centres are numerically possible;
   183	the safe enumerator must keep both `omega_j=1` and `omega_j=A_j`.
   184	
   185	Can a `(10)` level carry several orbits of the same multiplicity? Yes. Moh's
   186	condition for one selected nonzero factor is only `A_j V_j <= Q_j`. If
   187	`ell A_j V_j <= Q_j`, `p(pi)` may contain `ell` distinct nonzero Galois orbits
   188	with the same multiplicity and different residue representatives. That does
   189	not change the orbit size of any one disc; it only changes the packet count and
   190	degree consumed. At `s=3`, the knapsack budget captures this exactly for
   191	nonzero packets. At `s>3`, the parent-by-parent nesting remains open.
   192	
   193	Verdict on claim (2): CONFIRMED. Promote the product formula per orbit; do not
   194	promote any statement that product formula forces a single orbit or full
   195	geometric realizability of the packet.
   196	
