   198	
   199	The p.188 image is decisive. Lines p.188:3-10 discuss conjugations of
   200	`k((t^{1/A}))` over `k((t))`, where `A` is the reduced denominator of
   201	`delta_1`. The polynomial under discussion is `g_sigma(pi)` of degree
   202	`n* V_2`; this is the polynomial of the roots inside the selected bottom disc
   203	`D_1`, not the parent polynomial whose roots are bottom discs. The same lines
   204	also give the identity `A | (n*+m*)V_2 - 1`. Lines p.188:11-28 derive the
   205	two alternatives leading to Moh's printed (12)/(13). P.201:39-47 explicitly
   206	introduces (12)/(13) only after "when `r=2`".
   207	
   208	Definition 5.1 also matches this reading: for `i=1`, `D_1` contains
   209	`(n/d_2)V_2=eV_2` roots of `g`; there is no assigned `V_1`. Thus `A_1` is the
   210	increment from `delta_2,...` down to the bottom radius `delta_1`, and it
   211	controls the internal polynomial of the `D_1` roots. It is not an additional
   212	Galois orbit multiplier for discs.
