288:It is **not** a theorem that the fibre has a single orbit of bottom-major discs. Distinct radii, or same-radius clusters whose residue coefficients are not in one cyclotomic orbit of the splitting field, give several orbits. Moh p.150 does not settle this. Quote, image: "Due to the existence of the automorphisms `Omega_i`, the tree data associated with the configuration of the roots of the equation (1) over `k[x]((eta))` are completely symmetric." The `Omega_i` are `eta |-> omega^i eta` on the **`eta`-adic** expansion of the curve `(f,g)` over `k(x)`. Different Galois group, different tree. CH:654–655 is right to refuse the import.
