   128	## 5. Item (5) — Galois (2.4)–(2.6), and the extra two-orbit control. **CONFIRMED** with hypothesis repair
   129	
   130	### 5.1 Re-derivation of (2.4)–(2.6)
   131	
   132	Let `Γ=μ_R` act by `z↦ωz`. Then `x=z^{-R}` is invariant, so `d/dx` commutes with `Γ`, and `γ(τ_i)=τ_{γi}` implies `γ(D_i)=D_{γi}` and `γ(R_i)=R_{γi}`. If primitives `F_i` exist in the Puiseux field `K=C((z))`, one has `γ(F_i)-F_{γi}∈C` because constants of `K` are `C`. This is an additive cocycle of the finite group `Γ` with values in `(C,+)`. In characteristic zero, `H^1(Γ,(C,+))=0`, so averaging
   133	
   134	```text
   135	F̃_i = (1/|Γ|) ∑_γ γ^{-1} F_{γ i}
   136	```
   137	
   138	produces equivariant primitives, hence `a_{γi}=a_i`. Consequently there is **one free integration constant per Galois orbit**.
   139	
   140	**Exact hypothesis for “one constant per orbit”.**
   141	
   142	1. The set `O` is a genuine orbit of a finite group of field automorphisms acting `C`-linearly (a `Γ`-orbit, or an orbit of a quotient through which `Γ` acts on a subfield). It need **not** be the full set of `n` branches, and the group need **not** be the full `μ_R`: averaging over `μ_R` or over the image group gives the same equivariant primitive, because the action on a proper subfield factors through a quotient and the extra summands repeat.
   143	2. Averaging over a set that is **not** Galois-closed does not apply. A geometric cluster that is not an orbit is not a legal input to (2.4).
   144	3. `NO-RESIDUE` is required to have primitives **in `K`**. It is **not** required for the count of free additive constants. In `K[log x]`, writing `H_i=H_i^P + c ρ_i log x`, the residue `ρ_i` is Galois-invariant on an orbit (one scalar equation per orbit, not a free constant), and the `z^0` terms of `H_i^P` still form an additive cocycle with `H^1=0`. The number of free constants remains `h=#orbits` with or without `NO-RESIDUE`; without it one cannot form `F_i` inside `K`.
   145	
   146	**Line repair for `SOL:319-327`.** Replace “Once `NO-RESIDUE` holds, primitives can be chosen equivariantly… averaging over the finite group” by: primitives in `K` exist iff `NO-RESIDUE`; given primitives in `K` or in `K[log x]` with a chosen additive Galois action, averaging over the finite Galois group of the common field (not necessarily the full `μ_R`, and not requiring the orbit to be all `n` branches) kills the additive cocycle. The free constant count is one per orbit either way.
   147	
   148	(2.5) is then immediate: each orbit sum `M_{r,O}` is `Γ`-invariant, so `[z^k]M_{r,O}=0` unless `R∣k`. Cancellation is internal to each complete orbit. Distinct orbits are not related by symmetry.
   149	
   150	(2.6) is the residual character of a disc stabilizer `π↦χπ` of order `Δ` acting on monic leading polynomials: `p_g(χπ)=χ^a p_g(π)`, `p_f(χπ)=χ^b p_f(π)`, hence supports in one residue class modulo `Δ`. A nonzero constant Wronskian forces `Δ∣a+b-1`. Simple roots give `a≡0` or `1 (mod Δ)`. These are star/Galois constraints, not extra copies of (DEG)/(POLY).
