+55db4a9ef9021ef28d3a86dbd3e44d5e3a5aa1b72d61ba23043497f78ba94411  branch-orbits-v2-grok46-20260903.md
+c61bb15528b25587578ac10101cb18267e83150fa6ea541e250f606e648d5418  branch-orbits-v2-review-gpt55-20260903.md
+d20bf0841a1ba2b229d423bb948e6c4474a4f5a83f55148071cae39cb6c506c2  moh_skeleton_full.py
+fb137b92d88b2f59f369bbffb2a0591aed69e9294135751c329da9eea58f6948  census-rebase-opus5-20260902.md
+```
+
+The report uses four types deliberately:
+
+- **PROVED-HERE** means an exact algebraic derivation, a rerun of frozen code,
+  or an exact statement about a matrix explicitly defined in this report.
+- **REVIEWED INPUT** means a promoted statement consumed from the charged
+  branch-orbit or star review, rather than reproved from Moh's pages here.
+- **UNREVIEWED / WITNESS-ONLY** means an extra completion chosen to make a
+  bounded experiment possible.  It is not inferred from the ray.
+- **OPEN** means the charged information is insufficient or the required
+  geometric theorem has not been proved.  Every OPEN is bounded and paired
+  with a cheapest test in Section 11.
+
+The numerical implementation of (1)--(13) is in `moh_skeleton_full.py:69-145`.
+The exact moment equations and decorated-input requirement are in
+`global-interpolation-sol56-20260902.md:161-199,459-570`; the hostile review
+confirms that even the tuple plus \((k,\text{orbit partition})\) is insufficient
+at `global-interpolation-review-grok46-20260903.md:207-229`.
+
+The D=105 lane was initially running, so none of its report or artifacts was
+read then.  Its `.run.v2` later acquired `final_status=DONE`; only after that
+transition was its sealed report read for the guarded comparison in Section 9.
+The prohibited ideation files were not read.  There were no ledger edits and no
+`jc2-lean` invocation.
+
+## 1. Symbolic verification of the ray
+
+### 1.1 Core skeleton and gcd chain
+
+Fix \(t\in\mathbf Z_{\geq0}\) and \(P=7t+6\).  The proposed data are
+
+\[
+ n=9P,\quad m=6P,\quad
+ M_1=-6P,\quad M_2=4P,\quad M_3=9P-2,
+\]
+
+with \(s=3\), \(V_2=1\), and \(V_3=6t+5\).  The order is strict:
+
+\[
+ M_2-M_1=10P>0,\quad
+ M_3-M_2=5P-2>0,\quad n-M_3=2.
+\]
+
+Also \(m<n\), \(M_1=-m\), and \(m\nmid n\), because \(n/m=3/2\).
+The gcd chain implemented at `moh_skeleton_full.py:76-82` is
+
+\[
+ d_2=\gcd(9P,6P)=3P,
+ \qquad d_3=\gcd(3P,4P)=P,
+\]
+
+and
+
+\[
+ d_4=\gcd(P,9P-2)=\gcd(P,2).
+\]
+
+Since \(P\equiv t\pmod2\), \(d_4=2\) for even \(t\) and \(d_4=1\) for
+odd \(t\).  Moreover \(d_s=P\geq6\), so the implemented numerical version of
+(6) holds.
+
+The two windows in (7), using the exact strict/non-strict predicate in
+`moh_skeleton_full.py:91-95`, reduce to
+
+\[
+ \frac P2 < 6t+5\leq P,
+ \qquad
+ \frac35 <1\leq 3(6t+5).
+\]
+
+Their four positive margins are respectively
+
+\[
+ \frac{5t+4}{2},\qquad t+1,qquad \frac25,qquad 18t+14.
+\]
+
+Thus every implemented window holds for every \(t\geq0\).
+
+There are two scope qualifications.  First, the historical printed version of
+(1) contains the search cap \(n\leq100\), so only \(t=0\) lies literally under
+that cap.  The frozen enumerator intentionally has no cap.  The infinite result
+is therefore a ray of the implemented numerical predicates, not an assertion
+that infinitely many entries lie inside Moh's bounded printed search.  Second,
+(3) and the geometric content of (4) concern a Jacobian pair and characteristic
+data; the scalar enumerator does not implement them.  This is exactly the
+scope distinction recorded at `n6-family-review-grok46-20260903.md:59-66`.
+
+### 1.2 Radii and increment denominators
+
+Using the exact product in `Skel._delta` (`moh_skeleton_full.py:84-89`) gives
+
+\[
+ \delta_3=1-\frac{n-M_3}{n-M_3-1}=1-2=-1.
+\]
+
+At the next level,
+
+\[
+ V_3(n-M_3)-d_3=2(6t+5)-P=5t+4,
+\]
+
+whereas
+
+\[
+ V_3(n-M_2)-d_3=(6t+5)5P-P=6P(5t+4).
+\]
+
+The quotient in the definition is therefore \(5/6\), so
+\(\delta_2=1/6\).  At the bottom, the two extra level-2 factors are
+
+\[
+ V_2(n-M_2)-d_2=2P,
+ \qquad
+ V_2(n-M_1)-d_2=12P.
+\]
+
+Combining all factors gives quotient \(5/12\), hence
+
+\[
+ \delta_1=7/12.
+\]
+
+For the increment denominators in (8),
+
+\[
+ A_2=\operatorname{den}(\delta_2)=6,
+\]
+
+while \(L_1=\operatorname{lcm}(\operatorname{den}\delta_3,
+\operatorname{den}\delta_2)=6\), and therefore
+
+\[
+ A_1=\operatorname{den}(L_1\delta_1)
+     =\operatorname{den}(7/2)=2.
+\]
+
+This uses the implemented definition at `moh_skeleton_full.py:98-107`; it
+does not confuse the absolute denominator of \(\delta_1\) with the increment
+denominator \(A_1\).
+
+### 1.3 Conditions (9)--(13), \(q\), and \(u\)
+
+At \(j=2\), condition (9) has
+
+\[
+ Q=V_3\frac{d_2}{d_3}=3(6t+5)=18t+15
+   =6(3t+2)+3.
+\]
+
+Thus \((\triangle_2,\square_2)=(3t+2,3)\).  Since \(V_2=1\),
+
+\[
+ V_2\leq\triangle_2
+\]
+
+and (10) holds, while \(V_2\not\equiv\square_2\pmod{A_2}\), so (11)
+fails.  The ray is uniformly **(10)-only**.
+
+At the bottom,
+
+\[
+ (n/d_2,m/d_2,V_2,A_1)=(3,2,1,2).
+\]
+
+The congruences implemented in `cond1213` show (12) false and (13) true:
+\(3\not\equiv0\pmod2\), while \(2\equiv0\pmod2\) and
+\(3-1\equiv0\pmod2\).
+
+Finally,
+
+\[
+ q=(1-\delta_1)\frac{de}{d+e}
+   =\frac5{12}\frac{2\cdot3}{5}=\frac12,
+\]
+
+where \((d,e)=(m/d_2,n/d_2)=(2,3)\), and
+
+\[
+ u=V_3d_2/d_3=18t+15.
+\]
+
+These are exact symbolic identities.  A SymPy check and the native `Skel`
+predicates agree for the four emitted members.
+
+### 1.4 Orbit packets and fixed \(N\)
+
+The reviewed nonzero-orbit law says that a (10) split has disc-orbit size
+exactly \(A_2\), not merely a divisor of it
+(`branch-orbits-v2-grok46-20260903.md:192-225`).  Since \(s=3\), the flat
+packing at the unique \(D_2\) is exact, not a relaxation
+(`branch-orbits-v2-grok46-20260903.md:247-265` and its review at
+`branch-orbits-v2-review-gpt55-20260903.md:498-503`).  One packet therefore
+has
+
+\[
+ |O|=6,\qquad w=|O|V_2=6,
+ \qquad c=|O|V_2q=3.
+\]
+
+Because
+
+\[
+ u=18t+15=6(3t+2)+3,
+\]
+
+the number \(r\) of such packets may range from \(1\) to \(3t+2\).  Thus
+
+\[
+ k=6r,\qquad N=3r,
+\]
+
+and the exact orbit-admissible set is
+
+\[
+ N_{\rm orb}=\{3,6,9,\ldots,3(3t+2)\}.
+\]
+
+Taking \(r=2\) yields \(k=12,N=6\) for every \(t\geq0\).  This proves the
+fixed-\(N\) numerical claim.  It does not prove attainment by a polynomial
+Jacobian pair.
+
