+## 1. Symbolic verification of the ray
+
+### 1.1 Core skeleton and gcd chain
+
+Fix \(t\in\mathbf Z_{\geq0}\) and \(P=7t+6\).  The proposed data are
+
+\[
+ n=9P,\quad m=6P,\quad
+ M_1=-6P,\quad M_2=4P,\quad M_3=9P-2,
+\]
+
+with \(s=3\), \(V_2=1\), and \(V_3=6t+5\).  The order is strict:
+
+\[
+ M_2-M_1=10P>0,\quad
+ M_3-M_2=5P-2>0,\quad n-M_3=2.
+\]
+
+Also \(m<n\), \(M_1=-m\), and \(m\nmid n\), because \(n/m=3/2\).
+The gcd chain implemented at `moh_skeleton_full.py:76-82` is
+
+\[
+ d_2=\gcd(9P,6P)=3P,
+ \qquad d_3=\gcd(3P,4P)=P,
+\]
+
+and
+
+\[
+ d_4=\gcd(P,9P-2)=\gcd(P,2).
+\]
+
+Since \(P\equiv t\pmod2\), \(d_4=2\) for even \(t\) and \(d_4=1\) for
+odd \(t\).  Moreover \(d_s=P\geq6\), so the implemented numerical version of
+(6) holds.
+
+The two windows in (7), using the exact strict/non-strict predicate in
+`moh_skeleton_full.py:91-95`, reduce to
+
+\[
+ \frac P2 < 6t+5\leq P,
+ \qquad
+ \frac35 <1\leq 3(6t+5).
+\]
+
+Their four positive margins are respectively
+
+\[
+ \frac{5t+4}{2},\qquad t+1,qquad \frac25,qquad 18t+14.
+\]
+
+Thus every implemented window holds for every \(t\geq0\).
+
+There are two scope qualifications.  First, the historical printed version of
+(1) contains the search cap \(n\leq100\), so only \(t=0\) lies literally under
+that cap.  The frozen enumerator intentionally has no cap.  The infinite result
+is therefore a ray of the implemented numerical predicates, not an assertion
+that infinitely many entries lie inside Moh's bounded printed search.  Second,
+(3) and the geometric content of (4) concern a Jacobian pair and characteristic
+data; the scalar enumerator does not implement them.  This is exactly the
+scope distinction recorded at `n6-family-review-grok46-20260903.md:59-66`.
+
+### 1.2 Radii and increment denominators
+
+Using the exact product in `Skel._delta` (`moh_skeleton_full.py:84-89`) gives
+
+\[
+ \delta_3=1-\frac{n-M_3}{n-M_3-1}=1-2=-1.
+\]
+
+At the next level,
+
+\[
+ V_3(n-M_3)-d_3=2(6t+5)-P=5t+4,
+\]
+
+whereas
+
+\[
+ V_3(n-M_2)-d_3=(6t+5)5P-P=6P(5t+4).
+\]
+
+The quotient in the definition is therefore \(5/6\), so
+\(\delta_2=1/6\).  At the bottom, the two extra level-2 factors are
+
+\[
+ V_2(n-M_2)-d_2=2P,
+ \qquad
+ V_2(n-M_1)-d_2=12P.
+\]
+
+Combining all factors gives quotient \(5/12\), hence
+
+\[
+ \delta_1=7/12.
+\]
+
+For the increment denominators in (8),
+
+\[
+ A_2=\operatorname{den}(\delta_2)=6,
+\]
+
+while \(L_1=\operatorname{lcm}(\operatorname{den}\delta_3,
+\operatorname{den}\delta_2)=6\), and therefore
+
+\[
+ A_1=\operatorname{den}(L_1\delta_1)
+     =\operatorname{den}(7/2)=2.
+\]
+
+This uses the implemented definition at `moh_skeleton_full.py:98-107`; it
+does not confuse the absolute denominator of \(\delta_1\) with the increment
+denominator \(A_1\).
+
+### 1.3 Conditions (9)--(13), \(q\), and \(u\)
+
+At \(j=2\), condition (9) has
+
+\[
+ Q=V_3\frac{d_2}{d_3}=3(6t+5)=18t+15
+   =6(3t+2)+3.
+\]
+
+Thus \((\triangle_2,\square_2)=(3t+2,3)\).  Since \(V_2=1\),
+
+\[
+ V_2\leq\triangle_2
+\]
+
+and (10) holds, while \(V_2\not\equiv\square_2\pmod{A_2}\), so (11)
+fails.  The ray is uniformly **(10)-only**.
+
+At the bottom,
+
